18.13.m:
“Now the impulse of vigor,” thus six [verses].
May all Maruts today, all with help, may all kindled fires be [with us];
may all gods come here with help, may all wealth be [ours] in this contest for vigor.
“Unto me of vigor…”
“Now of vigor…”
May vigor from the seven quarters, or from the four distances,
may vigor protect me here with all the gods in winning wealth.
May vigor impel the gift to me today, may vigor make the gods grow through oblation;
for in the impulse of vigor it bows down; as lord of vigor may I win all regions.
Vigor in front and in the middle of us, may vigor fashion the gods with the seasons;
for in the impulse of vigor it bows down; as lord of vigor may I become all regions.
I unite myself with the milk of the earth, I unite myself with waters, with plants;
so may I win vigor from Agni.
Milk on earth, milk in the plants, milk in heaven, in the intermediate space put milk;
rich in milk may the quarters be for me.

18.14.m:
Conquering by truth, whose abode is truth, Agni is the gandharva;
his apsarases are the plants, called Delights;
may he protect this our priesthood and nobility;
to him svāhā, vaṭ!
To them svāhā, vaṭ!
Very gracious, sun-rayed, Soma is the gandharva;
his apsarases are the asterisms, called Bekuris;
may he protect this our priesthood and nobility;
to him svāhā, vaṭ!
To them svāhā, vaṭ!
Joined, having all sāmans, the Sun is the gandharva;
his apsarases are the rays, called Swift;
may he protect this our priesthood and nobility;
to him svāhā, vaṭ!
To them svāhā, vaṭ!
Feeding, well-winged, the sacrifice is the gandharva;
his apsarases are the priestly fees, called Praises;
may he protect this our priesthood and nobility;
to him svāhā, vaṭ!
To them svāhā, vaṭ!
Prajāpati, the all-maker, Mind is the gandharva;
his apsarases are the ṛk-verses and sāmans, called Desires;
may he protect this our priesthood and nobility;
to him svāhā, vaṭ!
To them svāhā, vaṭ!
Vigorous, all-encompassing, the Wind is the gandharva;
his apsarases are the waters, called Strengths;
may he protect this our priesthood and nobility;
to him svāhā, vaṭ!
To them svāhā, vaṭ!
O lord of the world, whose dwellings are above, O lord of wide rule!
Bestow great shelter upon this priesthood, upon this nobility, svāhā!
You are the ocean, vaporous, wet-dropping;
bringing welfare, bringing delight, blow towards me, svāhā!
You belong to the Maruts, the host of Maruts;
bringing welfare, bringing delight, blow towards me, svāhā!
You desire help, you are active;
bringing welfare, bringing delight, blow towards me, svāhā!

18.15.m:
I yoke Agni with might, with ghee, the heavenly eagle, great in vigor;
by him may we fly to the summit of the ruddy one, ascending light in the highest firmament.
These two wings of yours, unaging, winged, with which you strike away the rakṣases, O Agni,
with both may we fly to the world of the virtuous, whither went the ancient sages of old.
The drop, the skilled falcon, possessing truth, the golden-winged bird, active,
great, firmly seated in the assembly;
homage be to you;
do not harm me!
You are the head of heaven, the navel of the earth, the sap of waters and plants;
all-lived, broad shelter, homage to the path! Resting on the head of all, you stand above.
In the ocean is your heart, within life; grant water, cleave the water-holder;
from heaven, from Parjanya, from the intermediate space, from the earth,
from there protect me with rain.

18.16.m:
May the seasons make you grow, O Agni, the years, the sages, what things are true;
shine together with heavenly radiance, illuminate all directions of the earth.
Be fully kindled, O Agni, and awake him; rise up for great good fortune;
and let not your worshiper come to harm, O Agni; may your priests be renowned, not others.
These Brahmins choose you, O Agni; be kind to us in our dwelling, O Agni;
slaying rivals, O Agni, conquering plotters, watch heedfully in your own home.
Fix wealth right here, O Agni; let not the humblers humble you, who were piled before;
may your domain be easy to manage, O Agni; may your worshiper grow unvanquished.
Take hold, O Agni, with your own dominion; strive in alliance with a friend, O Agni;
shine here, O Agni, to be invoked by many for the central position among kinsmen and kings.
Beyond insults, beyond enmities, beyond ignorance, beyond ruin today,
for do you cross over all perils, O Agni, and then give us wealth accompanied by heroes.
Invulnerable, Jātavedas, unvanquished, widely ruling, supporting dominion, shine here, O Agni;
freeing mortals from all diseases, guard us all around today with kindly [powers] for increase.
When you freed him from the realm of the beyond, from Yama's curse, O Bṛhaspati,
may the two Aśvins, the physicians of the gods, push back death from him by their powers, O Agni.
Looking upward from darkness to the higher light,
to the god among the gods, the Sun, the highest light, have we come.
O Bṛhaspati, O Savitṛ, awaken him; sharpen further him who is already sharp;
make him grow for great good fortune; may all the gods rejoice in him.
Supporting dominion, Agni, whose brilliance is unblunted, possessing a thousandfold [strength], may he shine when kindled;
upon the height of the earth, Jātavedas ascended to the intermediate space, he went to heaven.

18.19.m:
He wipes the juhū ladle.
The juhū is indeed the head of the sacrifice;
he takes hold of the head of the sacrifice itself.
With the juhū the gods indeed called the Virāj;
that is the juhū-nature of the juhū.
Agni is indeed the Virāj;
he thus calls Agni, the Virāj.
The meters departed from the gods, desiring a portion at the sacrifice, saying: 'We will not carry the offering for you who have no shares.'
To them they gave this [ghee] taken in four ladlefuls: for the introductory verse, for the offering verse, for the deity, for the vaṣaṭ-call.
Because he offers with four ladlefuls,
he makes the meters possessors of portions.
He offers [mantras] to Savitṛ,
for instigation.
It indeed departs from the head of the sacrifice if that which does not belong to Agni is performed at the beginning of the sacrifice.
When, having taken [it] eight times, he offers these eight,
the gāyatrī has eight syllables, Agni is connected with the gāyatrī;
thereby it does not depart from the head of the sacrifice.
For instigation,
[the mantras] to Savitṛ.
'They harness the mind'—these contain the mind;
for the mind is harnessed in the beginning;
by the mind the sacrifice is extended.
'Agni is light'—this contains light.
Savitṛ indeed brought together this light of the earth;
whatever of it was lowered here, that he brings together by this.
There are six verses;
six indeed are the seasons;
he establishes himself firmly in the seasons.
There are two yajuses;
two-footed is the sacrificer;
for firm support.
If he should desire: 'I would furnish the deity with the sacrificial renown through the oblation,' he should place a verse at the end;
he furnishes the deity itself with the sacrificial renown through the oblation.
If he should desire: 'I would furnish the sacrificer with the sacrificial renown through the oblation,' he should place a yajus at the end;
he furnishes the sacrificer himself with the sacrificial renown through the oblation.
'Prosper the chant through the verse'—
for prosperity.
'The Rathantara by the Gāyatra, the Bṛhat having the Gāyatra as its course'—
this [earth] is indeed the Rathantara, that [heaven] is the Bṛhat.
By stepping along with the forward step of the oblation through attainment, the sacrificer becomes wealthier;
by stepping along with the backward step of the oblation, he becomes worse off.
Reciting all of them together at once, he should offer with the last [formula];
he steps along with the forward step of the oblation itself, he becomes wealthier.
He should offer with each one individually if he should desire: 'May the sacrificer be uniform.'
He makes the yajuses of various potencies.
These are eight, the oblation is the ninth;
he makes the head of the sacrifice threefold;
for threefold is the head of the sacrifice.
Standing upright, he offers;
for standing upright, one is more full of virility.
He offers with a full ladle;
full is Prajāpati;
he obtains Prajāpati himself.

19.1.m:
Om.
With verses to Savitṛ he takes the spade,
for impulsion.
This [earth] is the gāyatrī, the intermediate space the triṣṭubh, the sky the jagatī, the directions the anuṣṭubh;
with the meters he turns it toward himself from these worlds.
It is sharpened on both sides.
With a plowshare sharpened on only one side, indeed, this much food is produced.
Food is Arka; Arka is Agni;
for the securing of food, of Arka.
He should make it a cubit long.
The sacrifice is measured by man;
[he makes it] measured by the sacrificial man.
He should make it a fathom long;
so great is the vigor in man;
[he makes it] measured by vigor.
He should make it of unmeasured [length],
for the securing of what is unmeasured.
He should make it from a tree that bears fruit;
this is the most vigorous of trees,
for the sake of [possessing] vigor.
He should make it spotted, of bamboo, hollow.
Agni departed from the gods;
he entered bamboo.
He put on these armors which are the joints.
He moved through this world because it is hollow.
Wherever he dwelt, that he burned out, and that became spotted.
Because it is spotted, of bamboo, and hollow,
it is most connected with Agni,
for prosperity,
and also for securing whatever [part] of him is hidden in it.

Agni departed from the gods;
wherever he went, Prajāpati looked after him.
The horse belongs to Prajāpati.
When they go with a horse,
[it is] for the discovery of Agni.
By this, indeed, the gods overcame the Asuras at the end.
When they go with a horse,
[it is] for the overcoming of the rival.
The demons did not surpass this one;
when they go with a horse,
[it is] so that the demons do not surpass [them].
They lead the horse in front, the donkey behind,
for the separation of the worse from the better;
therefore the worse follows behind the virtuous one going in front.
He gathers with a donkey;
therefore among animals putting forth seed this one is the youngest equally,
for Agni burned out its seed.
Because he gathers nourishment and Arka into this [earth] with it,
therefore this one is the longest-lived upon her.
"Run forward swiftly, O courser; harness the donkey, you two," [he says];
this refers to the horse and donkey.
"In every yoke, in every prize, we call Indra, the stronger friend, for help," [he says];
for help, for the prize, indeed, Agni is piled.
"Come hastening forward, trampling upon curses," [he says];
abundant indeed is the rival of him who thrives;
he thrives who piles Agni.
The horse is a thunderbolt;
sin, curse, is the rival;
with the thunderbolt indeed he tramples upon sin, the rival.
"In Rudra’s overlordship of the troops, being salutary, come," [he says];
animals belong to Rudra;
having begged the animals from Rudra for himself, he performs the rite.
"Traverse the wide intermediate space," [he says];
for he has the intermediate space as his deity at this time.
"Together with Pūṣan as yoke-mate," [he says];
Pūṣan indeed is the guide of paths;
for attainment.
"From the seat of the earth bring Agni, of the earth-mound, like the Aṅgirases," [he says];
Agni's dwelling is the earth-mound; the Aṅgirases gathered him at first with the deities;
he gathers him with his dwelling, with the deities.
"Let us approach Agni of the earth-mound like the Aṅgirases," he should say to whomever he meets;
every man has Agni;
he deprives him of his prize.
Having announced to Prajāpati, Agni should be piled;
having announced to the king,
for he is closest to Prajāpati.
This [earth] is Prajāpati;
her ear is the ant-hill;
when he addresses [the earth] having dug up the mound of the ant-hill,
he piles Agni having announced it to Prajāpati himself.
They hear of him having piled Agni: "So-and-so has piled Agni";
for it is an ear;
he speaks to the ear.

19.3.m:
"Agni has looked along the forefront of the dawns," [he says],
for discovery.
"The prize-winner having reached the path shakes off all combatants," [he says];
with this [verse] he drives away combatants.
"Having stepped upon the earth, O prize-winner, seek Agni with thy light," [he says];
Prajāpati sought him with the previous [verse] and found him with the following one;
he seeks with the previous one and finds with the following one.
"The sky is thy back, the earth thy dwelling-place," [he says].
The horse belonging to Prajāpati is indeed armed with the thunderbolt;
with its hair [it surpasses] animals with teeth on both jaws, with its teeth those with teeth on one side;
with the thunderbolt indeed he holds down the rival.
Where the counterpart of the sacrifice is performed, there counterpart animals are born.
"Step forth! He has stepped forth," with two counterpart [formulas] he makes [him] step forth;
therefore counterpart animals are born;
with two;
the sacrificer has two feet,
for firm standing.
Whatever oblation the Adhvaryu offers where there is no fire, the Adhvaryu becomes blind,
and the demons smite the sacrifice.
Agni desired the wives of Varuṇa;
his fiery splendor flew away;
it became gold.
When he throws down gold and offers,
he offers in what possesses Agni,
for success;
the Adhvaryu does not become blind,
and the demons do not smite the sacrifice.
"I besprinkle Agni with thought, with ghee," [he says];
thought is swifter than speech;
with thought indeed he reaches the oblation.
"Dwelling opposite all the worlds," [he says];
therefore he dwells facing toward all creatures.
"Finding the widest, impetuous food," [he says];
he makes food sweet for him.
"I besprinkle thee with speech, with ghee," [he says];
he repeats what has just been uttered before.
"Mayest thou enjoy it with a mind free of demons," [he says],
for the driving away of the demons.
"Agni of youthful splendor, of desirable appearance," [he says];
he places respect in him.
With two [verses] he offers;
the sacrificer has two feet,
for firm standing.
At each head of the sacrifice, indeed, the demons wish to slay the sacrifice.
When he traces around,
[it is] for the driving away of the demons.
With three [verses] he traces around;
three are these worlds;
from these worlds he drives away the demons.
"Around the prize-lord, the poet," with a gāyatrī he traces around;
the gāyatrī is the brahman;
with the brahman he encloses it.
"Around thee, O Agni, we as a fortress," with an anuṣṭubh;
speech is the anuṣṭubh;
the anuṣṭubh encompasses all meters;
with speech he encloses all meters.
"Thou, O Agni, with days," with a triṣṭubh;
vigor is the triṣṭubh;
with vigor he encloses it.
With an anuṣṭubh he traces around in the middle;
speech is the anuṣṭubh;
he places speech in the middle;
therefore speech speaks in the middle.
Fiery splendor is the gāyatrī,
the sacrifice is the anuṣṭubh,
sensory power is the triṣṭubh;
with fiery splendor and sensory power he encloses the sacrifice on both sides in himself.

19.4.m:
With two [verses] to Savitṛ he digs,
for impulsion.
He finds smoke with the previous [verse], light with the following one;
with two;
the sacrificer has two feet,
for firm standing.
"Thou art the back of the waters, the womb of Agni," [saying this] he takes the lotus leaf;
for this is the back of the waters, the womb of Agni.
With the lotus leaf he collects [the earth];
with his own womb he collects him,
for appeasement,
so that he will not burn through.
With a black antelope skin he collects [it];
the black antelope skin is the sacrifice;
with the sacrifice he collects the sacrifice,
so that it will not spill;
for the sacrifice does not spill in the sacrifice.
This indeed is the form of the brahman, namely the black antelope skin;
with the brahman and with the ṛc and sāman he collects him.
"Ye are shelter and ye are armor," [saying this] he spreads them;
the lotus leaf is this [earth], the black antelope skin that [sky];
these two indeed he thus spreads out.
"Both unrent and thick," [he says];
for both of these are unrent and thick.
"Capacious," [he says];
therefore these two are capacious.
"Bear the purīṣya Agni," [he says];
for he is purīṣya.
"Dwell together, O finders of light, turned toward each other in your self with your chest," [he says];
therefore these two, being distinct, face each other.
"About to bear within you Agni, full of light, never failing," [he says];
he places in him never-failing light.
"Thou art purīṣya, all-supporting," [he says];
for he is purīṣya, all-supporting.
"Atharvan first churned thee forth, O Agni," [he says];
Prajāpati is Atharvan;
Prajāpati churned him in the beginning;
Prajāpati indeed generates him.
"And thee, Dadhyañc the ṛṣi," [he says];
Dadhyañc Ātharvaṇa was full of fiery splendor;
with him he collects him.
"And thee, Pāthya the bull," [he says];
he repeats what has just been uttered before.
With four [verses] he heaps [the earth] together;
four are the meters;
with the meters he heaps him together.
With gāyatrī [verses] he should heap together for a brāhmaṇa;
for the brāhmaṇa belongs to the gāyatrī;
with triṣṭubh [verses] for a rājanya;
for the rājanya belongs to the triṣṭubh;
with jagatī [verses] for a vaiśya;
for the vaiśya belongs to the jagatī;
according to each one's meter.
With both he should heap together for one whom he might desire to become better off;
fiery splendor is the gāyatrī, sensory power the triṣṭubh;
he places fiery splendor and sensory power united in him.
With eight [verses] he heaps together;
the gāyatrī has eight syllables;
Agni belongs to the gāyatrī;
as great as Agni is, so great he collects him.
"Sit down, O Hotṛ," [he says];
he causes the deities to sit down in him.
"Down, O Hotṛ," [he says];
[he causes] men [to sit down].
"Sit down together," [he says];
[he causes] birds [to sit down].
"For he was born noble at the forefront of the days," [he says];
he generates gods and men seated together in him.

19.5.m:
"Release the divine waters rich in sweetness," [he says],
for the establishment of the plants.
"May the plants, well-fruited, spring up from their place of standing," [he says];
therefore the plants bear fruit.
"May Vāyu Mātariśvan unite [thee]," [he says];
therefore Vāyu bears the rain.
Vāyu is breath;
he places breath in him.
"To him, O god, let there be vaṣaṭ for thee," [he says].
There are six seasons;
he places rain in the seasons;
therefore it rains season by season.
If he were to say "vaṣaṭ" perceptibly, the vaṣaṭ-call would become exhausted of power;
if he were not to say "vaṣaṭ", the demons would destroy the sacrifice.
"To him, O god, let there be vaṣaṭ for thee," [he says];
[uttered] imperceptibly, the vaṣaṭ-call does not become exhausted of power,
[and] the demons do not destroy the sacrifice.
If he were to offer with clarified butter, he would afflict the earth with burning heat.
He pours out water,
for appeasement, so that [the earth] will not burn through.
"Well-born with light," [he says];
now indeed he is born, when he is collected;
just when he is born, he places light in him.
"He has attained a sheltering protection, the light of heaven," [he says];
sheltering protection is the sacred word (brahman);
he establishes him in the sacred word.
"O Agni, radiant one, put on a multiform garment," [he says].
The meters are Agni's garment;
he clothes him with the meters.
The anuṣṭubh is Agni's dear body;
he clothes him with his dear body.
He who knows thus becomes a possessor of clothing.
Bound up, this [bundle] is indeed Varuṇa's wrath.
"Stand up, O thou of fair sacrifice; erect for our aid," [he says];
he impels Varuṇa's wrath upwards.
With two [verses];
the sacrificer has two feet,
for firm standing.
"Being born, thou art the embryo of the two rodases," [he says].
These two [worlds] are the two rodases;
he is the embryo of these two;
he establishes him in these two.
"O Agni, lovely, distributed among the plants," [he says];
therefore Agni is along all the plants.
"Go forth from the mothers, neighing loudly," [he says].
The plants are indeed his mothers;
he leads him forth away from them.
"Be firm, strong-limbed," [he says];
he places firmness in the donkey;
therefore this one among the beasts is the best bearer of burdens.
"Be friendly to human offspring, O Aṅgiras," [he says].
Now he would scorch the offspring;
[this is] for appeasement.
"Do not scorch the sky and earth, nor the atmosphere, nor the trees," [he says];
he pacifies him towards these worlds.
"Let the prize-winner go forth, neighing loudly," [he says],
for attainment.
"Let him not fall before his life-span," [he says];
he places life in him;
therefore the donkey reaches the full span of life;
therefore, when a donkey dies, they are afraid.
"A bull bearing the bull Agni," [he says];
for as a bull he bears the bull.
"The embryo of the waters, oceanic," [he says];
for he is the embryo of the waters, oceanic.
"O Agni, come to the feast," [he says];
with Agni the gods spread this out in the beginning,
for enjoyment.
Deprived of his abode, not yet having reached firm standing, he now meditates upon the sacrificer and the Adhvaryu.
"Truth, order; truth, order," [he says];
this [earth] is order (ṛta), that [sky] is truth (satya);
he establishes him in these two.
"O plants, receive this Agni, auspicious, coming here towards you," [he says].
The plants are his share;
he unites him with them;
moreover, in those [plants] from which he displaced him, he establishes him.
"Flowering, well-fruited," [he says];
therefore the plants bear fruit.
With two [verses] he brings [it] down;
the sacrificer has two feet,
for firm standing.
"With his glare," [he says], and loosens [the bundle];
he unties Varuṇa's wrath.
"Waters, you are indeed refreshing," [saying this] he brings the waters;
the waters are pacified;
with the pacified [waters] he pacifies his heat.
With three [verses];
threefold is Agni;
as great as Agni is, so great is the heat of him he pacifies.
He mixes [the clay] with goat's hair.
A goat is Agni's dear body;
he mixes him with his dear dear body.
With gravel,
for firmness.
He mixes [it] with potsherds from a ruined site;
he afflicts the wild beasts with burning heat;
if he were to mix [it] with [potsherds] from a village, he would afflict domestic beasts with burning heat;
therefore these [wild beasts] are the smallest among the beasts that are born,
for they are stricken with heat.

19.6.m:
"Mitra having united the earth," [he says];
this [earth/pot] is indeed Varuṇa's wrath;
with Mitra indeed he approaches Varuṇa's wrath.
"The Rudras having united the earth; united by the Vasus, by the Rudras," [he says];
with these deities Prajāpati indeed united it;
with them he unites it.
With some [materials] he holds out, with others he recites formulas;
he forms a pair;
and he secures the blessing of the sacrifice from the center.
"Thou art the head of Makha," [he says];
Makha is indeed the sacrifice;
he makes the head of the sacrifice.
"May the Vasus make thee with the gāyatrī metre," [he says];
with these deities Prajāpati made it;
with them he makes it.
He makes it three elevations high;
three are these worlds;
he attains these worlds.
He makes it five elevations high;
fivefold is the sacrifice;
he secures the sacrifice.
"Hold offspring in me," [he says];
this is the prayer, the milking of the metres.
He should make it a cubit in size;
the sacrifice is commensurate with man;
[it is made] commensurate with the sacrificial man.
He should make it as large as he can encompass with both arms;
this much is the virility in man;
[it is made] commensurate with virility.
He should make it a span in size;
this much is the mouth;
Agni is the mouth of the gods;
[it is made] commensurate with the mouth.
He should make it without measure;
for the securing of the unmeasured.
He should make it with four nipples;
for the milking of Aditi;
with six nipples,
for the milking of the seasons;
with eight nipples,
for the milking of the gāyatrī;
with two nipples,
for the milking of heaven and earth.
For sorcery he should make it nine-cornered;
the thunderbolt is threefold;
he hurls the thunderbolt at his rival.
"Thou art Aditi's girdle; may Aditi take thy rim," [saying this] he makes the rim with a yajus;
for humans make it without a yajus;
they distinguish it from a human vessel.
If it should break before baking, the sacrificer would suffer distress, his sacrifice would be destroyed.
"Having made the great ukhā," [he says];
he hands it over to the deities.
"May the Vasus fumigate thee with the gāyatrī metre," [he says];
with these deities Prajāpati fumigated it;
with them he fumigates it.
He fumigates it with horse dung;
the horse belongs to Prajāpati, Prajāpati is the sacrifice,
for having the same origin.
He fumigates with seven [formulas];
the ukhā is the head of the sacrifice;
seven are the vital breaths;
he places the breaths in the head;
therefore there are seven breaths in the head.

19.7.m:
"The divine Aditi belonging to all the gods," [he says];
this [earth] is Aditi;
with Aditi he digs into Aditi,
so as not to hurt her;
for one's own does not harm one's own.
"May the wives of the gods [place] thee," [he says];
formerly the wives of the gods placed it here;
with them he places it.
"May the Dhiṣaṇās [kindle] thee," [he says];
the Dhiṣaṇās are knowledge;
with knowledge he kindles it around.
"May the Varutrīs [cook] thee," [he says];
the Varutrīs are the Hotrās;
with the Hotrās he cooks it.
"May the Gnās [bake] thee," [he says];
the Gnās are the metres;
with the metres he bakes it.
"May the wives with undamaged wings [bake] thee," [he says];
the wives are the spouses of the gods; they baked it originally;
with them he bakes it.
"May they bake it twice," he says;
therefore grain ripens twice a year.
He bakes it with six [formulas];
six indeed are the seasons; with the seasons the wives of the gods baked it;
with them he bakes it.
This [ukhā], when kindled around, is Varuṇa's wrath;
"Of Mitra who supports the peoples," [he says];
with Mitra he approaches Varuṇa's wrath.
"May god Savitṛ unearth thee," [saying this] he unearths it with a Savitṛ verse,
for impulsion.
"Unwavering on earth, fill the quarters and directions," [he says];
therefore Agni shines through all directions.
"Arise, become great, stand upright, be firm," [he says];
he makes it firm.
If it should break, the sacrificer would suffer distress, his sacrifice would be destroyed.
"O Mitra, I entrust this ukhā to thee," [he says];
Mitra is the sacred power;
to the sacred power he entrusts it.
If it should break, he should unite [the fragments] with the potsherds and make another;
that indeed is the atonement of the sacrifice.
"May the Vasus pierce thee with the gāyatrī metre," [he says];
with metres it is made, with metres it is fumigated, with metres it is baked;
the metres pierce the metres by their own abode.
A vessel that is not pierced belongs to the asuras;
when he pierces it,
he places it among the gods.
He pierces it with goat's milk;
for she is most connected with Agni;
for prosperity,
and also with the highest milk.

19.9.m:
He pours out an [offering] on eleven potsherds to Agni and Viṣṇu.
Agni is indeed all deities;
Viṣṇu is the sacrifice;
he takes hold of both the deities and the sacrifice.
Agni is the mouth of the gods;
Viṣṇu is the furthest end;
he has grasped together the two ends of the sacrifice.
Then there is a caru in ghee for the Ādityas.
The Ādityas, going from here as the highest, went to that world.
These creatures are of the Ādityas, nearest to the Ādityas;
he approaches his own deity.
It is in ghee;
for the Ādityas have ghee as their portion.
Then there is [a cake] on twelve potsherds for Agni Vaiśvānara.
The year is indeed Agni Vaiśvānara;
twelve-monthed is the year;
[it is] for the attainment of the year.
Whoever piles the fire without a womb, [that fire] enters into the womb of the sacrificer;
it burns him up.
The year is indeed Agni Vaiśvānara;
the year is the womb of Agni.
In that it is for Agni Vaiśvānara,
he piles it with its womb.
This is indeed the dear body of Agni, namely the Vaiśvānara one.
In that it is for Agni Vaiśvānara,
having made an oblation for his dear body, he receives the dear body.
Agni Vaiśvānara is indeed desire.
In that he pours out [an offering] for Agni Vaiśvānara,
he attains that desire for which desire it is given.
In that it is on twelve potsherds,
twelve-monthed is the year;
the creatures that fall away, fall away from the year; those that are established, are established in the year;
having found a firm standing in the year, he bears the fire.

19.10.m:
He offers six Ādhīta-yajus formulas.
There are six seasons;
he consecrates him by means of the seasons;
by means of the seasons he raises up its vigor.
He offers seven of these;
there are seven vital breaths;
he consecrates him by means of the vital breaths.
He offers [them] separately;
therefore the vital breaths have different potencies: the eye, the ear, speech.
He offers the last one with an anuṣṭubh;
speech is the anuṣṭubh;
he places speech as the highest;
therefore speech, highest of the vital breaths, speaks what is articulated.
The anuṣṭubh is all metres;
cattle are metres;
cattle are food;
he secures cattle, food.
In that he heats [the pan] in the flame, by that he secures what has been;
in that [he heats it] on embers, by that what is to be.
It should be heated on embers;
for what is to be is greater than what has been.
"Do not break well, do not suffer damage," [he says];
he firmly establishes it.
"Rise up uninjured in this sacrifice," [he says],
for attainment.
He heats it with two [formulas];
the sacrificer is two-footed;
[this is] for support.
[Fire] should be kindled around for one wishing to prosper;
the consecrated is an embryo;
he comes to be from here, as it were;
from here he generates him.
Having churned [the fire], he should place it down for one who has attained prosperity;
for he has come to be;
he approaches his own deity.
Bringing it from elsewhere, he should place it down for him regarding whom he might desire, "May I produce a rival for him";
he produces a rival for him.
He should place it down from an oven for one desiring food;
food is made in an oven;
he secures food in its own birthplace.
He should place it down from a tree that burns above for one desiring heaven;
this [fire] among the fires leads to heaven;
[this is] for the attainment of the heavenly world.
"Whose food is wood, whose libation is ghee," [saying this,] he places down a scraped kṛmuka stick after anointing it with ghee;
Agni's dear body, with it he entered the kṛmuka;
ghee is fiery splendor;
he anoints his dear body with fiery splendor.
He places down reeds;
reeds are vigor;
he bestows vigor upon him.
"From the further region," [with this verse he places down] an udumbara [stick];
the udumbara is vigor;
he bestows vigor upon him.
"From the furthest distance," [with this verse he places down] a vaikaṅkata [stick];
when Agni was created, his brilliance reached the vikaṅkata;
he secures that very [brilliance].
"Whatever [sticks], O Agni," [with this verse he offers];
formerly Agni would not burn what was not cut with an axe;
the ṛṣi Prayoga made that savory to him with these [verses];
in that he places down kindling-sticks with these [verses],
he makes the kindling savory to him;
his food becomes savory, who knows thus.
The metres are Agni's womb;
in that he places down [the sticks] with the metres,
he maintains him in his own womb.
"Night after night bringing [it] without slackening," [he says];
he invokes a blessing.
"Kindling Agni on the navel of the earth," [he says];
by this he conquers armies.
"The marching hosts that," [with this verse he places a stick];
when Agni was born, the demons wanted to kill him;
those gods saw these verses;
with them they beat off the demons from him;
in that he places down kindling-sticks with these,
[it is] for striking down the demons.
Then this Mālimlava hymn [is used];
the gods and the asuras contended;
those gods saw this Mālimlava hymn;
with it they overcame the asuras;
in that he places down a kindling-stick with this,
[it is] for the overcoming of the rival.
Therefore, in the year they pile the fire, they are prone to seize a thief.
"The marching hosts that," [with this verse he places] an udumbara [stick];
where the gods distributed vigor, thence the udumbara arose;
he bestows vigor upon him when just born.
"With two tusks, O Agni, the robbers," [with this verse he places] an aśvattha [stick];
this is the vanquisher of rivals among trees;
[it is] for victory.
When he says, "With two tusks," he should meditate in mind upon him whom he hates;
mind is swifter than speech;
he presents him as an oblation to Agni.
"The robbers who are among men," [with this verse he places] a vaikaṅkata [stick];
when Agni was created, his brilliance reached the vikaṅkata;
he secures that very [brilliance].
"He who acts as an enemy towards us," [with this verse he places] one made of śamī;
[it is] for appeasement.
"Who scorns us and wishes to harm," [he says];
therefore one should not speak ill of the fire-builder.
"Sharpened is my prayer," [he says];
by prayer he sharpens dominion,
by dominion prayer;
therefore dominion having prayer as its domestic priest surpasses other dominions;
therefore prayer possessed of dominion surpasses other brahmins.
"I have raised up their arms, their luster, and their strength," [he says];
he invokes a blessing.
"I destroy the enemies with prayer, I raise up my own," [he says];
according to the yajus formula.

19.11.m:
"Visible, the gold disk has shone afar," [with this] he should put on the gold disk.
Agni is death; gold is immortality;
by immortality alone he shields himself from death.
It has twenty-one knobs,
for firm standing.
There are twenty-one worlds of the gods;
from them alone he repels the rival.
He should place the knobs on the outside;
from this world alone he repels him.
Fastening it on, he casts it around on both sides;
from both worlds indeed he excludes him.
"Night and Dawn," [he says];
by Day and Night alone he encompasses him.
"The wealth-giving gods maintained Agni," [he says];
the vital breaths indeed are the wealth-giving gods;
by the vital breaths alone he has maintained him.
The sling has six cords;
there are six seasons;
by the seasons alone he encompasses him.
It has twelve cords;
twelve months are the year;
by the year alone he encompasses him.
It is made of muñja-grass;
muñja-grass is vigor;
with vigor he encompasses him.
He should not carry it below the navel;
Agni would burn up his semen.
He should carry it above the navel;
above the navel is divine;
Agni is all deities;
in what is divine alone he carries the deities.
"[Assuming] all forms," with [this verse] to Savitṛ he puts it on,
for impulsion.
Sitting, he puts it on;
therefore sitting, offspring are brought forth.
"You are the eagle, winged," [he says];
these are Agni's requisites;
with these he brings Agni together;
this indeed is Agni's dear domain;
that alone he secures.
"Go to the sky; fly to the sun," [he says],
for the attainment of the heavenly world.
These worlds belonged to the Asuras.
The gods said to Viṣṇu: "As far as this youth strides, grant us so much!"
In one stride he strode across this [earth], the gāyatrī his metre; in one stride across the intermediate space, the triṣṭubh his metre; in one stride across the sky, the jagatī his metre; in one stride across the directions, the anuṣṭubh his metre.
The gods found these worlds of the Asuras;
thence the gods came to be, the Asuras were defeated.
He who, knowing thus, steps the strides finds these very worlds of his rival;
he himself flourishes; his rival is defeated.
"Agni has cried," [he says];
by this Vatsaprī Bhālandana secured Agni's dear domain;
by this alone he secures Agni's dear domain.
"O Agni, returning! O Agni, Aṅgiras! Together with vigor, with wealth!" with this he returns;
therefore domestic animals come home in the evening from the wilderness to the village.
With four [verses];
for animals are four-footed.
He turns around to the right;
therefore the right half of the body has greater vigor.
"I have brought you here," [he says];
for he brings him here.
"May all the clans desire you; fix dominion firmly upon us," [he says];
whomever he desires: "May he have dominion!" he should think of him in mind;
he indeed attains dominion.
"Loosen the highest bond from us, O Varuṇa," [he says];
Śunaḥśepa Ājīgarti, seized by Varuṇa, saw this [verse];
by it he was freed from Varuṇa's bond;
by it he loosens Varuṇa's bond.
"High on top of the dawns, tall he has stood," [he says];
darkness is evil;
by it he beats away evil.
"The swan dwelling in light," [he says];
by it he gratifies the companions.
"The real," [he says];
orderly indeed he establishes him upon the real.
He sets it upon a chair;
he who is borne in the fire-pan is consecrated among the Agnis;
therefore he sets it upon a chair.
If he were to set it below, embryos would fall out;
he sets it above,
for the retention of embryos,
to prevent falling out.
With three [verses] he attends [it];
there are three breaths: prāṇa, vyāna, apāna;
them alone he places in the sacrificer.
Three are these worlds;
in these worlds alone he prospers.

19.12.m:
Now this is the Vātsapra [hymn].
By this, indeed, Vatsaprī Bhālandana obtained Agni's dear domain.
By this he obtains Agni's dear domain.
By this, indeed, he conquered the superior speech of the ṛṣis;
by this he conquers the evil superior speech.
By this, indeed, he conquered curses.
He who, knowing thus, worships with this, conquers curses.
In that he strides the strides,
thereby he sustains setting out on a journey.
In that he worships,
thereby [he sustains] peaceful dwelling.
On the previous day he strides;
on the following day he worships.
Therefore, the mind of some creatures is on journeying, that of others on peaceful dwelling.
Therefore, the wanderer rules over peaceful dwelling;
therefore, the wanderer settles upon what belongs to peaceful dwelling.
It is eleven [verses];
the triṣṭubh has eleven syllables;
the triṣṭubh is vigor;
he obtains vigor.
In the past, our [cart] had a single pole;
then the ṛṣis saw this two-poled setting up for Agni.
Therefore, they make a two-poled setting up for Agni.
In imitation of that, a two-poled cart is made here.
"Up may all the gods [bear] you," [he says];
all the gods are the vital breaths;
by the vital breaths he raises him up.
All the gods are men;
by men he raises him up.
"O Agni, let them bear [you] with [their] thoughts," [he says];
for whichever thought he raises him up, with that he makes him prosper.
"Go forth, O Agni, shining," [he says];
indeed, at that time he causes burning to creatures;
[this is] for appeasement.
"Agni has roared," [with this] he should recite over [it] if it should creak;
for indeed, they carry with a cart that groans;
therefore, the owner of a cart and the owner of a chariot are the most honored among guests.
"O lord of food, give us of [your] food," [he says];
he makes food savory for him.
"Free from sickness, vigorous," [he says];
"free from consumption," he says by this.
"Further promote the giver; place strength in our two-footed and four-footed [creatures]," [he says];
he invokes a blessing.
"Honor Agni with a kindling-stick," [saying this] he places the kindling-stick [on the fire], having anointed it with ghee;
just as one cooks [a dish] containing ghee for a Brahmin guest, even so is that.
With a gāyatrī he should place it for a Brahmin;
for the Brahmin belongs to the gāyatrī;
with a triṣṭubh for a Rājanya;
for the Rājanya belongs to the triṣṭubh;
with a jagatī for a Vaiśya;
for the Vaiśya belongs to the jagatī;
according to the respective metre.
"O divine waters, receive this ash," [saying this] he scatters the ash into the waters.
The waters are Agni's womb;
he makes him go to his own womb.
With three [verses];
Agni is threefold;
as great as Agni is, to that extent he makes him go to a firm standing.
He indeed casts Agni away who scatters the ash into the waters;
"having united with the mothers, shining, sit down again," [he says];
he places light in him.
He indeed is deprived of strength and cattle who scatters the ash into the waters;
"again with strength, together with wealth," [he says];
again he obtains strength and cattle.
"Be mindful of this speech of mine, O youngest [god]," [he says];
therefore, creatures having fallen asleep awaken.
"Be mindful, O patron, O bounteous one," [he says];
the cattle, having wandered having set out, coming back, sit down according to their place.
With two [verses];
the sacrificer is two-footed;
for firm standing.

20.2.m:
Now [as for] these Nairṛta [bricks]:
[there are] three, black, baked with chaff.
In that they are black,
this colour, indeed, clings to him whom Nirṛti seizes.
This is indeed the share of Nirṛti.
In that there is chaff,
by appearance and by share he appeases Nirṛti.
"Beyond this darkness..." he takes [them] with [a verse] addressed to Vaiśvānara;
he sweetens [them].
They carry [them] to this [southwestern] direction.
To this direction, indeed, they carry him whom Nirṛti seizes.
This is Nirṛti's direction;
in her own direction he appeases Nirṛti.
He lays [them] down on a natural barren spot;
this, indeed, of it is seized by Nirṛti.
Where it is seized by Nirṛti, there he appeases Nirṛti.
He lays [them] down pointing away;
he appeases Nirṛti pointing away.
He lays down three;
threefold arranged, indeed, is man.
As great as his self is, from that he appeases Nirṛti.
"Which the goddess Nirṛti bound on you..." with these words he casts down the sling;
the bond, indeed, belongs to Nirṛti;
he frees him from Nirṛti's bond.
To the world of the fathers, indeed, go those who proceed to the south with the Nairṛta [bricks];
the Āhavanīya is the world of the gods.
When he worships the Āhavanīya,
he turns back to the world of the gods.
He worships with a triṣṭubh;
the triṣṭubh is vigour;
he places vigour in him.
With a single [verse];
singly he places vigour in him.

20.5.m:
He recites in a whisper.
Unexpressed, indeed, is Agni at this time, when he is unseparated;
therefore he recites in a whisper.
He recites the metres.
By means of the metres, indeed, Agni reached the high altar;
therefore he recites the metres.
They traverse the course of the sun when they make the horse step forward and backward;
therefore that sun goes east and goes west.
The horse belongs to Prajāpati.
When he makes the horse step,
he builds it with Prajāpati himself.
"You are the back of the waters, the womb of Agni," [with this] he lays down the lotus leaf.
In its own womb he builds it,
for pacification, for not burning through.
"Growing, great, and [spreading] out, O lotus..." [he says];
for he who comes into being grows.
"Extend with the measure of heaven in breadth," [he says];
he extends it.
"Brahman born first in the east..." [with this] he lays down the golden disk.
With the creatures having Brahman at their head, Prajāpati prospered;
[it is] for prosperity.
It has twenty-one knobs,
for firm standing.
Twenty-one are the worlds of the gods;
from them he repels the rival.
He should make the knobs below,
for the subjugation of the rival.
Agni is not to be built on earth, nor in the intermediate space, nor in heaven.
If he were to build [it] on earth, he would burn up the plants with heat;
if in the intermediate space, the birds;
if in heaven, heaven.
Gold is immortality.
When he lays down the golden disk,
he builds Agni in immortality itself.
"The Golden Germ came into being in the beginning..." [with this] he lays down the golden man.
By this he has maintained the world of the sacrificer;
moreover, he builds Agni with light in the centre.
"The drop has spilled..." [with this] he touches [it];
he firmly establishes it in the Hotrās, in truth.
He attends [it] with the serpent-heads;
he hands it over to death itself;
moreover, whatever brilliance is in the serpent, that he obtains.
If he were to lay [them] down, he would be liable to die.
If he were to lay [them] down turned toward each other with the other heads, the domestic animals would be biting;
if turned away from each other, the wild [animals].
He should only speak the Yajus formula;
by that alone he obtains that brilliance;
thereby it is pacified.
Therefore, formerly, they did not wish to look upon him who builds the fire-altar,
for he has obtained all these brilliances: that which is in fire, that in the serpent, that in the sun.
Now, this rakṣas-slaying hymn of Vāmadeva is at the head of the sacrifice;
at the head of the sacrifice, indeed, the rakṣases wish to destroy the sacrifice;
[it is] for the slaying of the rakṣases.
It consists of five verses;
Agni is fivefold;
from as great as Agni is, he slays the rakṣases.
He offers, moving around all the directions;
from the directions indeed he slays the rakṣases.
He lays down two offering-ladles;
he lays down these two [worlds] indeed.
He lays [them] down silently;
for one should not obtain these two with a Yajus formula.
The man is the trunk, the two offering-ladles are the two arms;
when he lays down the two offering-ladles,
[it is] for having a body.
On the south, indeed, the rakṣases ate the offerings of the gods, carrying [them] off;
they screened them with kārṣmarya [wood].
In that he lays down [a ladle] made of kārṣmarya on the south,
[it is] for screening from the rakṣases.
He fills [it] with ghee.
The kārṣmarya is a vajra, ghee is a vajra;
in a vajra he places a vajra.
He lays [it] down with the Gāyatrī;
Agni is connected with the Gāyatrī, having the Gāyatrī metre;
with his own metre he causes him to prosper.
He fills the [ladle] of udumbara wood with curd.
Curd is food, the udumbara is vigour;
in food he places vigour.
He lays [it] down with the Triṣṭubh;
the Triṣṭubh belongs to Indra, Indra's power is food;
he obtains Indra's power, food.
This [earth] is the one made of kārṣmarya; that [heaven] is the one made of udumbara.
When he lays down the udumbara [ladle] to the north,
therefore that [heaven] is north of this [earth].
[The verse] contains the word 'head' [mūrdhan];
a head is made by these two.
Agni is to be built in the Virāj;
the offering-ladle is the Virāj;
when he lays down the two offering-ladles,
he builds Agni in the Virāj itself.

20.6.m:
Whatever brick an unknowing Adhvaryu lays down first, with that he blocks the breath of the sacrificer, and of his offspring and of his domestic animals.
It is a naturally perforated [brick],
for the release of the breaths,
for the beholding of the heavenly world.
The horse belongs to Prajāpati.
When he makes the horse smell it,
he builds it with Prajāpati himself.
This [earth] indeed is the naturally perforated [brick];
it is this [earth] that he thus lays down.
If he should think, "My rival has surpassed me in front," he should push it forward to the east;
whichever rival of his has surpassed him in front, him he drives forward with this.
If he should think, "My rival is behind me," he should push it away to the west;
whichever rival is behind him, him he drives away with this.
He drives away a superior rival;
he repels an inferior [rival].
If he should think, "He is equal to me," he should shake it.
Whatever offspring this [earth] shook off, they perished;
this [earth] shakes him off.
Now this is the dūrvā-brick.
Agni is a domestic animal;
domestic animals do not delight in a place without pasture.
He thus makes an abode for the domestic animals,
for the retention of domestic animals.
"Growing up knot by knot...", [with this he lays it down]
for establishment.
"With a thousand and with a hundred...", [he says];
possessing a thousand, it is commensurate with Prajāpati.
He attains to a thousandfold nourishment of domestic animals, who, knowing thus, lays it down.
Now this is the Vāmabhṛt [brick].
By this, indeed, the gods took away the desirable wealth, the cattle, of the Asuras.
He takes away the desirable wealth, the cattle, of his rival by this.
It has two Yajus-formulas.
He takes away the world of his rival with that which has two Yajus-formulas;
for the rival is in the second world.
It has a golden head;
endowed with golden light he goes to the heavenly world.
Two glass beads are fastened symmetrically;
therefore the two eyes are symmetrical.
Now these two are the seed-pourers.
That [sky] indeed is Svarāj; this [earth] is Virāj.
Into a woman lying on her back the man pours seed;
that [sky] pours seed into this [earth];
this [earth] brings forth.
Agni consumes.
He builds Agni possessing seed;
he propagates offspring and consumes food, who, knowing thus, lays these two down.
"You are Svarāj," [with this] he lays it down on the south;
therefore the man lies beside the woman on the south.
If he should build for one in the earlier stage of life, he should lay down both together in the first layer;
turned toward each other they pour seed for him.
He should lay down either one for him whom he hates;
he deprives him of seed itself.
If he should build for one in the later stage of life, he should lay down one in the first layer, the other in the topmost layer;
he encloses the seed once poured by means of these two.
He lays down two seasonal [bricks],
for the maintenance of the seasons.
He lays down two each,
for the seasons are in pairs.
Now this is the thrice-marked [brick].
The gods and the Asuras did exactly the same in the sacrifice.
Whatever the gods did, that the Asuras did.
Those gods saw this thrice-marked [brick].
They laid it down with the mark upward;
then they overcame the Asuras;
thence the gods flourished, the Asuras perished.
He who, knowing thus, lays this down with the mark upward, it is for the non-overcoming by his rival; he himself flourishes, his rival perishes.
He should lay it down with the mark downward for him whom he hates;
he makes him follow the womb of the Asuras into ruin.
These worlds indeed are thrice-marked;
he attains these worlds by it.
From the clay with which they make the ukhā-pot, of that he should make this;
thereby all his bricks become possessed of light, possessed of Yajus-formulas.

20.7.m:
The tortoise is the sacrificial essence of domestic animals.
When he lays down the tortoise,
the domestic animals attend upon him, recognizing this very sacrificial essence.
He lays it down alive,
for he is fit for sacrifice.
He has four feet;
there are four directions;
he establishes [himself] in the directions themselves.
"Great heaven and earth for us...", [he says];
by this he holds the forms of heaven and earth.
He anoints it with curds and with honey.
Honey is the sap of waters and plants;
when he anoints it with honey,
he secures the sap of waters and plants.
Curds is the sacrificial essence of domestic animals, [their] vigour;
when he anoints it with curds,
he places the sacrificial essence, vigour, into the domestic animals.
One Agni is built with bricks, another is built with cattle;
when he lays down the living tortoise,
thereby he makes him built with cattle.
He is the topmost step to the heavenly world;
just as one who knows the terrain, being knowledgeable, leads others directly, even so he leads him to the heavenly world.
Agni is to be built in the navel of Viṣṇu.
Whoever builds Agni without a navel, [Agni] enters the navel of the sacrificer;
he burns him up.
This is the navel of Viṣṇu,
when he lays down the mortar;
he builds Agni in the navel of Viṣṇu.
It is made of udumbara wood;
udumbara is vigour;
he places vigour in the middle, in the sacrificer and in his offspring;
therefore vigour invigorates offspring from the middle.
It is a span in size;
for so great is the navel of Viṣṇu.
He lays it down with a [formula] dedicated to Viṣṇu and suitable for ritual action;
for this ritual action is being performed.
Now this is the ukhā-pot.
Ojas and virility are collected
when the ukhā-pot [is placed];
he secures ojas and virility.
He secures the light of these worlds;
he places light in the middle;
he builds Agni with light in the middle;
therefore they worship the light from the middle.
He fills it with sand, with its own abode;
sand is the ashes of Agni Vaiśvānara;
by its form he secures Vaiśvānara.
If he should lay it down deficient, the sacrificer would become hungry;
he lays it down full;
the sacrificer becomes not hungry;
he eats inexhaustible food.
He fills it with ghee;
ghee is the dear domain of Agni;
with his dear domain he makes him prosper.

20.9.m:
Agni is an animal.
This womb of the animal is prepared.
The Apasyā bricks are semen.
When he lays down the Apasyā bricks,
he places semen in the womb.
When the animal develops [in the womb], then it is born.
The Chandasyā bricks are cattle.
When he lays down the Chandasyā bricks after the Apasyā bricks,
[it is] for the propagation of cattle.
He lays down five [of each].
Cattle are fivefold.
He propagates the animal in its full measure.
This [earth] feared the excessive burning of Agni.
She created these Apasyā bricks.
She laid them down.
When the Apasyā bricks are laid down,
[it is] for her non-burning.
She indeed said: "He whose [altar] has these laid down shall eat food through the priesthood."
He who, knowing thus, lays these down eats food through the priesthood.
He lays down five in the east, facing west;
therefore an animal is born in the east facing west.
[He lays down] five in the south, facing north;
therefore the male lies beside the female from the south.
[He lays down] five in the west, facing east;
therefore semen is deposited from the west towards the east.
Five Chandasyā bricks are in the north.
The Chandasyā bricks are cattle; cattle have their abode in the north.
He gathers the cattle, when born, toward their own abode,
for the non-injury of the cattle.
He should not lay down the Apasyā bricks in the north;
if he were to lay them down, Varuṇa would seize the offspring with dropsy.
After the Apasyā bricks, he lays down the Prāṇabhṛt bricks.
Once the semen is poured, he places in it breath, mind, sight, hearing, and speech.
Therefore the animal is born breathing, seeing, hearing, and speaking.
Those containing the word 'breath'—"This one in front is earth..."—he lays down in the east;
he places breath in the front;
therefore an animal breathes forward.
Those containing the word 'mind'—"This one in the south is Viśvakarman..."—[he lays down] in the south;
he places mind in the south;
therefore the right half [of the body] possesses more mind;
therefore they attend to the mind from the south.
Those containing the word 'sight'—"This one in the west is Viśvavyacas..."—[he lays down] in the west;
he places sight in the west;
therefore an animal sees looking forward.
Those containing the word 'hearing'—"This light in the north..."—[he lays down] in the north;
he places hearing in the north;
therefore an animal hears more from the north.
Those containing the word 'speech'—"This thought above..."—[he lays down] in the middle;
he places speech in the middle;
therefore speech speaks from the middle.
He lays them down crosswise;
therefore cattle walk moving their limbs crosswise.
With those he laid down in the east, Vasiṣṭha prospered;
with those in the south, Bharadvāja [prospered];
with those in the west, Jamadagni [prospered];
with those in the north, Viśvāmitra [prospered];
with those in the middle, Viśvakarman [prospered].
He who thus knows their prosperity prospers.
He who thus knows their arrangement, for him it is arranged.
He who thus knows their connection becomes possessed of connections.
He who thus knows their foundation becomes possessed of a foundation.
He who thus knows their abode becomes possessed of an abode.
He who thus knows their firm foundation attains a firm foundation.
Possessed of a foundation, possessed of an abode becomes he who knows thus.

20.10.m:
Now these [are the] Āśvinī [bricks].
This Agni is indeed a disrupted sacrifice.
Who knows that? Whatever of this is not performed, whatever is not piled, that he arranges with these, that he heals.
He lays down five of these.
Agni is fivefold;
as much as Agni is, so much medicine he provides for him.
With these he arranges the directions of Agni.
"On the back of Aditi I seat thee," [he says];
with this he supports these worlds.
He lays down the seasonal [bricks];
for the arrangement of the seasons.
He lays down five [of them];
five indeed are the seasons;
as many as the seasons are, so many he arranges.
They have identical beginnings, they have identical conclusions;
for the seasons have identical beginnings, have identical conclusions.
He differentiates [them] with a single vyāhṛti;
therefore the seasons differentiate.
Therefore the seasons of the same year have varied forms.
He lays down the seasonal [bricks] after the Āśvinī [bricks];
when the seed has been deposited, he lays down the seasons;
therefore, when the seed has been deposited, [the embryo] develops in accordance with the seasons.
He lays down the Vāyavyā [bricks] after the seasonal [bricks];
he places the breath into the seasons;
therefore these, going round equally, do not decay;
therefore the wind blows following the seasons.
He lays down the Apasyā [bricks] after the Vāyavyā [bricks];
therefore the wind carries the rain;
he secures the rain by means of these.
If he were to lay them down together, it would rain for one season.
He sets them down moving around;
therefore it rains season by season.
He lays down the Vayasyā [bricks] after the Apasyā [bricks];
the Vayasyā [bricks] are cattle;
when he lays down the Vayasyā [bricks] after the Apasyā [bricks],
therefore cattle have different minds and different habits;
they are of one mind regarding water alone.
Having laid down the Apasyā [bricks], he should lay down the Vayasyā [bricks] for him whom he desires: "May he have cattle."
Waters are the concord of cattle;
he piles Agni in the concord of cattle; he becomes possessed of cattle.
Having laid down the Vayasyā [bricks], he should lay down the Apasyā [bricks] for him whom he desires: "May he be without cattle."
He piles Agni in the discord of cattle; he becomes without cattle.
He lays down four in the front;
therefore in front an animal is thinner;
therefore the eye has four forms: two white, two black.
They contain the word 'head';
therefore in the front is the head of animals.
Five on each side;
for the unification of the animal;
therefore an animal slopes forward.
"The he-goat is strength," he lays down on the south;
"The ram is strength," on the north;
he lays down his shoulders.
"The tiger is strength," on the right wing;
"The lion is strength," on the left wing;
he places vigour in the wings.
"The tiger is strength," the eastern one;
"The lion is strength," the western one;
therefore the tiger is born in the east, the lion in the west.
"Man is strength," in the middle;
therefore man is the lord of animals.

20.11.m:
"O Indra and Agni, do ye two make firm the unshakeable brick," [he says];
Indra and Agni are vigour and prowess.
This layer is the intermediate space;
the intermediate space is loose, as it were;
creatures are the intermediate space;
he places vigour and prowess in creatures.
It is naturally perforated;
for the emission of the breaths,
for the sighting of the heavenly world.
The horse belongs to Prajāpati;
when he causes the horse to smell [it],
he piles him by means of Prajāpati.
Now these [are the] direction-bricks.
When the gods were going to the heavenly world, their directions collapsed;
they saw these direction-bricks;
with them they made firm the directions.
When these are laid down,
[it is] for the support of the directions.
"Thou art the queen, the eastern direction," [he says];
therefore this is the queen of directions.
"Thou art the sovereign, the southern direction," [he says];
therefore this shines among directions.
"Thou art the imperial ruler, the western direction," [he says];
therefore here is imperial rule.
"Thou art the self-ruler, the northern direction," [he says];
therefore here is self-rule.
"Thou art the supreme ruler, the upward direction," [he says];
therefore this is the supreme ruler of directions.
He who, knowing thus, lays these down, becomes all these.
"Protect my life," [with this] he lays down ten in front;
nine indeed are the breaths in a man, the navel is the tenth;
he places the breaths in him.
"Bestow light upon me," [with this he lays down] the last one;
he places light as the highest;
therefore speech is the highest light among the breaths.
He lays down ten of these;
the virāj consists of ten syllables;
the virāj is the light among metres;
the eye is light;
he places the eye in front of creatures.
"The mā metre," he lays down on the south;
therefore the months turn to the south.
"The earth is the metre," in the west;
for firm establishment.
"Agni is the deity," in the north;
Agni is vigour;
he places vigour on the north;
therefore one who advances from the north conquers.
He lays down twelve on the south, twelve in the west, twelve in the north;
they amount to thirty-six;
the bṛhatī consists of thirty-six syllables;
the bṛhatī attained self-rule among metres;
he who, knowing thus, lays these down, attains self-rule.
These are thirty-six;
the bṛhatī consists of thirty-six syllables;
cattle belong to the bṛhatī; the intermediate space
is this layer; cattle are the intermediate space;
he places cattle in cattle.
Certain breaths have the Ādityas as their abode; others have the Aṅgirases as their abode.
Those in front have the Ādityas as their abode;
those in the back have the Aṅgirases as their abode.
"Thou art the head, the ruler," he lays down in front;
those breaths that have the Ādityas as their abode, he held them thereby.
"Thou art the controller, the ruler," in the back;
those breaths that have the Aṅgirases as their abode, he held them thereby.
He lays down seven in front, seven in the back;
therefore the lower breath has half a share of the upper breaths.
Prajāpati, having created creatures, desired regarding them: "May I be the head."
He saw these Mūrdhanyā [bricks]; by means of them he rose up;
he became their head.
That is the headship of the Mūrdhanyā [bricks].
He who, knowing thus, lays these down, becomes the head among his equals.

20.12.m:
"The swift one, the trivṛt," [with these words] he lays down [a brick] in front.
The trivṛt is indeed the face of the sacrifice;
he arranges the face of the sacrifice in front.
"The firmament, the saptadaśa," [with these words he lays down one] on the south.
The saptadaśa is indeed food;
food propagates after the seventeen-month year.
Food comes to be on the south;
therefore a man eats food with his right hand.
Because he lays down [a brick] containing the saptadaśa on the south,
therefore birds fly following along with the right wing.
[He lays down a brick] containing the ekaviṃśa on the west,
for firm standing;
for birds rest upon [their tail] behind.
[He lays down a brick] containing the pañcadaśa on the north.
The pañcadaśa is indeed vigour;
he places vigour on the north;
therefore he who advances from the north conquers.
"Agni's share art thou, the overlordship of the consecration," [he says];
in front are the two trivṛt [formulas];
he arranges [them] in regular succession in the face of the sacrifice.
[He lays down a brick] containing the pañcadaśa on the south, [and a brick] containing the saptadaśa on the north,
for the unification of the two wings.
The saptadaśa is indeed food;
because he lays down [bricks] containing the saptadaśa on both sides,
he places food on both sides;
therefore a man eats food, grasping it with both hands.
It contains the ekaviṃśa,
for firm standing.
"Agni's share art thou, the overlordship of the consecration," [he says];
this and that the gods won by means of these [bricks];
this and that he wins by means of these;
these bricks are named Spṛts.
"With one they praised; creatures were disposed," [he says];
this and that the gods created by means of these [bricks];
all this he secures by means of these.
This [arrangement] is arranged following the arrangement of the Arkya;
food is arka; Agni is arka;
he eats food for whom, knowing thus, this is arranged.

20.13.m:
The gods and the Asuras did indeed perform equally at the sacrifice.
Whatever the gods did, that the Asuras did.
Those gods saw this fourth layer;
they, reciting it in one way, laid it down in another way.
The Asuras did not follow after that.
Then the gods flourished, the Asuras were defeated.
He who, knowing thus, lays down this fourth layer, it is for the non-pursuit of his rival; he flourishes in himself, his rival is defeated.
"The swift one, the trivṛt," [with these words] he lays down [a brick] in front.
The trivṛt is indeed the face of the sacrifice;
he places the face of the sacrifice in front.
"The firmament, the saptadaśa," [with these words he lays down one] on the south.
The saptadaśa is indeed food;
he secures food on the south.
[He lays down a brick] containing the ekaviṃśa on the west,
for firm standing;
[a brick] containing the pañcadaśa on the north.
The pañcadaśa is indeed vigour;
he places vigour on the north;
therefore he who advances from the north conquers.
"Forward-rushing, the aṣṭādaśa," [with these words he lays down one] in front;
there are two trivṛt [formulas];
he places [them] in regular succession in the face of the sacrifice.
"Overcoming, the saviṃśa," [with these words he lays down one] on the south.
The saviṃśa is indeed food;
he secures food on the south.
"Splendour, the dvāviṃśa," [with these words he lays down one] on the west.
In that it is two:
the sacrificer is two-footed;
[it is] for firm standing.
In that it is twenty:
two Virājs [of ten syllables]; the Virāj is food;
he stands firmly in the Virāj, in proper food.
"Heat, the navadaśa," [with these words he lays down one] on the north;
therefore the left of the two hands has more heat; it contracts the arm.
"The womb, the caturviṃśa," [with these words he lays down one] in front.
The caturviṃśa is indeed the face of the sacrifice;
he places the face of the sacrifice in front.
"Embryos, the pañcaviṃśa," [with these words he lays down one] on the south.
The pañcaviṃśa is indeed food;
he secures food on the south.
In that it is ten:
the Virāj has ten syllables; the Virāj is food;
he stands firmly in the Virāj, in proper food.
In that it is fifteen:
the sacrifice is fifteenfold;
he places a vajra on high,
for the slaying of the Rakṣases.
"Vigour, the triṇava," [with these words he lays down one] on the west.
These worlds are the triṇava;
he stands firmly in these worlds.
"Gathering, the trayoviṃśa," [with these words he lays down one] on the north;
therefore the left of the two hands gathers more.
"Will, the ekatriṃśa," [with these words he lays down one] in front.
The ekatriṃśa is indeed speech;
he places speech in the face of the sacrifice.
"The summit of the ruddy one, the catustriṃśa," [with these words he lays down one] on the south.
Yonder sun is indeed the summit of the ruddy one;
yonder sun is spiritual lustre;
he secures spiritual lustre on the south.
"Support, the trayastriṃśa," [with these words he lays down one] on the west.
Thirty-three are the deities;
he stands firmly in the deities.
"The vault, the ṣaṭtriṃśa," [with these words he lays down one] on the north.
The vault is indeed the heavenly world;
he establishes the sacrificer in the heavenly world.

21.1.m:
"Thou art Agni's share, the overlordship of the consecration," [with these words] he lays down [a brick] in front.
Agni is indeed the face of the sacrifice; the consecration is the face of the sacrifice; the trivṛt is the face of the sacrifice.
He places the face of the sacrifice in front.
"Thou art the share of the men-watchers, the overlordship of the creator," [with these words he lays down one] on the south.
Those who are wise are the men-watchers; food is the creator; generation is the saptadaśa.
He covers food over for the creatures that have been brought forth;
therefore one who is born reaches out toward food.
"Thou art Mitra's share, the overlordship of Varuṇa," [with these words he lays down one] on the west.
Mitra is in-breathing; Varuṇa is out-breathing;
he establishes in-breathing and out-breathing in the sacrificer.
"Thou art Indra's share, the overlordship of Viṣṇu," [with these words he lays down one] on the north.
Indra is vigour; Viṣṇu is vigour; the pañcadaśa is vigour;
he places vigour on the north;
therefore he who advances from the north conquers.
"Thou art the Vasus' share, the overlordship of the Rudras," [with these words he lays down one] in front.
The Vasus are the face of the sacrifice; the Rudras are the face of the sacrifice; the caturviṃśa is the face of the sacrifice;
he places the face of the sacrifice in front.
"Thou art the Ādityas' share, the overlordship of the Maruts," [with these words he lays down one] on the south.
The Ādityas are food; the Maruts are food; the pañcaviṃśa is food;
he secures food on the south.
"Thou art Aditi's share, the overlordship of Pūṣan," [with these words he lays down one] on the west.
Aditi is a firm foundation; Pūṣan is a firm foundation; the triṇava is a firm foundation;
[it is] for a firm foundation.
"Thou art the divine Savitṛ's share, the overlordship of Bṛhaspati," [with these words he lays down one] on the north.
Bṛhaspati is the sacred word; the catuṣṣṭoma is the sacred word;
he places spiritual lustre on the north.
It contains the word 'Savitṛ';
therefore a gift sent forth towards the north [goes] to the brahmins.
"The support, the catuṣṣṭoma," [with these words he lays down one] in front.
The catuṣṣṭoma is indeed the face of the sacrifice;
he places the face of the sacrifice in front.
"Thou art the share of the barley-halves, the overlordship of the non-barley-halves," [with these words he lays down one] on the south.
The months are indeed the barley-halves; the months are food;
he secures food on the south.
"Thou art the Ṛbhus' share, the overlordship of all the gods," [with these words he lays down one] on the west.
The deities are thirty-three;
he establishes himself firmly in the deities.
"The revolution, the aṣṭācatvāriṃśa," [with these words he lays down one] on the north;
therefore the two hands are of equal strength.
This arrangement is arranged in accordance with the arrangement of the Arkya;
food is the arka; Agni is the arka;
he who knows thus, for whom this is arranged, eats food.
Those that are chief he lays down in front;
for him who knows thus, a chief [child] is born.
Those that contain food [he lays down] on the south;
for him who knows thus, an eater of food is born.
Those that contain a firm foundation [he lays down] on the west;
he who knows thus attains a firm foundation.
Those that contain vigour [he lays down] on the north;
for him who knows thus, a strong [child] is born.
He who knows thus the prosperity of these [bricks] prospers.

21.2.m:
"O Agni, thrust away our born rivals," [saying this,] he lays [a brick] down in the east.
Whichever rival has already preceded him, that one he thrusts away by it.
"[Thrust away those] born through force," [saying this, he lays one down] in the west.
Whichever rival is behind him, that one he thrusts away by it.
He thrusts away the better rival [in front];
he thrusts back the worse rival [behind].
"The forty-four-fold stoma," [he says,] on the south;
"The sixteen-fold stoma," on the north.
The sixteen-fold is a vajra;
the triṣṭubh is a vajra;
the forty-four-fold belongs to the triṣṭubh.
The vajra is grasped with the left [hand] and hurled to the south.
Having driven away the rival, he hurls the vajra after him.
"The forty-four-fold stoma," he lays down on the south.
The forty-four-fold is spiritual lustre;
he secures spiritual lustre on the south.
"The sixteen-fold stoma," [he lays down] on the north.
The sixteen-fold is vigour;
he places vigour on the north.
Therefore one who marches forward from the north conquers.
"Thou art the earth's rubbish," [saying this, he places one] in the middle.
The middle of the self is the rubbish;
he piles Agni having a self.
These are the bricks named rival-less;
he has no rival, who knowing thus lays them down.
Now these are the virājs;
he places the virāj as supreme among the cattle;
therefore one who has cattle speaks the supreme speech.
He lays them down ten by ten,
for unification.
The gods, indeed, going to the heavenly world—those metres of theirs which were unexpressed, belonging to the heavenly world,
with them they went to the heavenly world.
The ṛṣis wished to know after them;
they fashioned them with bricks.
These are those metres that do not convey the sacrifice.
Whichever unexpressed metres of the gods belong to the heavenly world, with them he goes to the heavenly world, who knowing thus lays them down.
Now these are the stomabhāgā [bricks].
Bṛhaspati brought this support of the sacrifice;
that these are laid down,
[is] for the firm support of the sacrifice.
Bṛhaspati collected this brilliance of the sacrifice;
that these are laid down,
he piles Agni possessed of brilliance.
Prajāpati created creatures;
he created them with the stomabhāgās.
That these are laid down,
[is] for procreation.
On the south, indeed, the sacrifice of the gods collapsed;
they propped it up with the stomabhāgās.
That these are laid down on the south pointing northward,
[is] for the propping up of the sacrifice.
He lays down seven by seven,
for having identical strength.
Three in the middle,
for firm support.
Now these are the nākasad [bricks].
By the nākasads the gods went to the heavenly world;
that these are laid down,
[is] for conquering the heavenly world.
Nothing is painful to one who has gone to that world: that is the nākasad-nature of the nākasads.
These are the bricks named all-prṣṭha;
whatever is the brick of the pṛṣṭhas, whatever brilliance of the pṛṣṭhas [there is], that he secures, who knowing thus lays them down.
Now these are the pañcacūḍā [bricks].
The wives are the domain of the sacrificer;
in that he lays the pañcacūḍās on the nākasads,
he establishes the sacrificer firmly in heaven.
These are the Apsarases;
having assumed this form, becoming wives, they lie beside the sacrificer in yonder world.
He lays one down in the west facing east;
therefore the wife sits behind facing east.
These are the protectors of the sacrificer's body;
he reaches full life, who knowing thus lays them down.
These deities belong to the heavenly world;
with them he goes to the heavenly world.
These deities are fit for sorcery;
whomever he hates, about him he should say, "I place that one in your jaws";
he cuts him off from these deities; he quickly perishes.
